{
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "route": {
    "route_id": "alexandria-cairo",
    "name": "Alexandria-Cairo Railway",
    "name_arabic": "سكة حديد الإسكندرية-القاهرة",
    "countries": "Egypt",
    "gauge_mm": 1435,
    "year_opened": 1854,
    "era": "historic",
    "color": "#b45309",
    "corridor_confidence": "high",
    "description": "1854; one of Africa’s oldest",
    "notes": "First railway in the Ottoman Empire, Africa and the Middle East; still the main Egyptian trunk.  [established] Alexandria–Kafr el-Zayyat opened 1854; through to Cairo 1856.  [route] Kafr el-Zayyat, Tanta and Banha are the well-documented original stops (high confidence). Sidi Gaber, Damanhour and Qalyub are main-line-corridor CANDIDATES (medium/low) — not explicitly confirmed on the original 1854-56 line; verify against historical maps.",
    "history": "~120 miles (193 km). Construction begun 1852 at the request of Khedive Abbas I; engineered by Robert Stephenson. Alexandria–Kafr el-Zayyat opened 1854; through to Cairo 1856. Crossed the Nile TWICE — at Kafr el-Zayyat and at Benha. The Kafr el-Zayyat crossing was first a car-float ferry; after a fatal accident on 15 May 1858 (a train carrying heir presumptive Ahmad Rifaat Pasha fell off the float and he drowned), Stephenson replaced it with a ~500 m swing/tubular bridge. By the mid-1930s, seven express trains ran each way daily (2.5–3.25 hours); Tanta was the principal intermediate junction.",
    "verification_status": "unverified",
    "source_ids": [
      "farnworth",
      "levantrain_system",
      "railwaywonders",
      "wp_enr"
    ]
  },
